{:edb/db-before-t 2851 :edb/db-after-t 2852 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536164 50 #inst "2026-10-09T17:45:15.169Z" 13194139536164 true] [17592186129204 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386810 13194139536164 true] [17592186129204 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386817 13194139536164 true] [17592186129205 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386824 13194139536164 true] [17592186129688 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536164 true] [17592186129688 1100 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T: (u, v, w) → (x(u, v, w), y(u, v, w), z(u, v, w))$, given that\n\n$u = 2x + y, v =-x + 3y - 2z, w = 3y + 4z$." 13194139536164 false] [17592186129688 1100 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=-x+3y-2z,\\,w=3y+4z.\n$$" 13194139536164 true] [17592186129688 1145 "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(12+6)-1(-4-0) \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536164 true] [17592186129691 1017 #edb/ref 17592186127940 13194139536164 false] [17592186129691 1017 #edb/ref 17592186386831 13194139536164 true] [17592186129691 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536164 true] [17592186129691 1100 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T$, where $T$ is defined as\n\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u^{2} - v \\\\ u^{2} + v \\\\ e^{w}\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139536164 false] [17592186129691 1100 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ {u}^{2}+v \\\\ {e}^{w}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139536164 true] [17592186129691 1145 "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={u}^{2}-v,\\,y(u,v,w)={u}^{2}+v,\\,z(u,v,w)={e}^{w}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & ={e}^{w}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2u & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {e}^{w}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{w}|\\begin{aligned}2u & -1 \\\\ 2u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{w}(2u+2u) \\\\  & =4u{e}^{w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 4u{e}^{w} & =0 \\\\ u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u=0$ defines the plane $u=0,$ i.e., $vw$-plane." 13194139536164 true] [17592186386810 1017 #edb/ref 17592186386811 13194139536164 true] [17592186386810 1089 "q-161800" 13194139536164 true] [17592186386810 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536164 true] [17592186386810 1100 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=z-y,\\,v=x+z,\\,w=-3x.\n$$" 13194139536164 true] [17592186386810 1113 #uuid "3af84c3d-711b-5e23-a347-b035f74ed8c8" 13194139536164 true] [17592186386810 1145 "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =-3 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(-1)|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(0+3)+1(0-0) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536164 true] [17592186386811 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386812 13194139536164 true] [17592186386811 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386813 13194139536164 true] [17592186386811 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386814 13194139536164 true] [17592186386811 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386815 13194139536164 true] [17592186386811 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386816 13194139536164 true] [17592186386811 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536164 true] [17592186386811 1041 "selection" 13194139536164 true] [17592186386811 1104 #edb/ref 17592186386815 13194139536164 true] [17592186386811 1110 #uuid "061b4845-3db8-5a6d-8a92-4c57275c9f39" 13194139536164 